.SH "INTERACTIVE VIEWER"
.B duscope view
opens the treemap in a viewer that takes the whole terminal.
It reads the newest saved scan that holds the path (the current directory
by default), or the saved scan
.B \-\-scan
names; an argument that is no path but
.B latest
or the first digits of a saved scan's id names that scan.
When no saved scan holds the path, it scans the path and saves the scan,
as the list verbs do:
.B \-\-no\-save
keeps that scan unsaved, and
.B \-\-no\-scan
fails instead.
.B \-\-color\-by
and
.B \-\-theme
set the colours it starts with; without a theme, the terminal's
background picks
.B light
or
.BR mocha .
.PP
.B duscope scan
.I PATH
.B \-i
scans first and opens the result, which is saved only with
.BR \-\-save .
The map takes the treemap flags of
.BR scan :
.BR \-\-depth ,
.BR \-\-min\-tile ,
.BR \-\-pool\-files ,
.BR \-\-color\-by ,
.B \-\-theme
and
.BR "\-\-treemap pixels" .
.PP
Both refuse to start, before any scan, with a usage error (exit 2), when
stdout is not a terminal (a pipe, a file,
.BR \-o ),
when the output is one for programs
.RB ( \-\-json ,
.BR \-\-plain ,
a format that
.B \-\-format
or
.B DUSCOPE_FORMAT
names other than
.B table
or
.BR treemap ,
or the agent and CI variables of ENVIRONMENT), or when the terminal is
smaller than 80\(mu24.
The viewer gives the terminal back as it found it when it ends: by
.B q
or
.BR Ctrl\-C ,
by an error, or by the signals
.B SIGTERM
and
.BR SIGHUP .
.SS Screen
The top row holds the scan's path, the four views and the status; the
second, the path from the scan's root to the selection.
The map fills the pane under them.
At 110 columns and wider, a column at its right holds three panes: the
selection's facts (its size, its counts, its shares of its folder and of
the scan, its type, when it was modified, its mode and owner); its
siblings, the entries of its folder, largest first or in the
.B s
sort, which reach entries too small to have a tile; and the legend, what
the colours stand for and how much of the map each takes.
Narrower, the facts fold into a pane under the map.
The last row is the key line: six keys for the view and the pane in
focus.
.PP
The selection starts at the largest file, reached through the largest
folder at each level.
The arrow keys move it to the neighbouring tile at the same depth;
.B Enter
goes down the path, back to where it went before or into the folder's
largest entry, and
.B Backspace
goes up it.
.B z
zooms the map to the folder under the cursor, and
.B Z
and
.B 0
zoom back out.
.B Tab
moves the arrow keys onto the siblings, then the legend.
.B i
grows the facts into everything known of the selection, from the scan and
from
.BR explain :
what deleting it would free, the command that regenerates it, what git
says of it.
A click selects the tile or the row under it (on a legend row, it filters
by the row), a double click zooms, and the mouse wheel goes up and down
the path.
.SS Views
.TP
.B 1
The treemap: a box for each folder, a tile for each file.
.TP
.B 2
The list: the entries of the selection's folder as a table, in the
.B s
sort.
.TP
.B 3
The largest files under the map's root, as
.B top \-\-files
lists them;
.B Enter
shows a row's file on the map.
.TP
.B 4
The types: the bytes of each file class under the map's root, as
.B by\-type
adds them up;
.B Enter
keeps that class's tiles on the map.
.SS Keys
.B ?\&
lists these keys over the panes, in this order.
.TP
.BR Left ", " Down ", " Up ", " Right ", " h ", " j ", " k ", " l
Move to the neighbouring tile, or along the list, the table or the pane in
focus.
.TP
.B Enter
Go down the path.
In the largest files, show the row's file on the map; in the types and the
legend, keep the row's tiles on the map.
.TP
.B Backspace
Go up the path; at the map's root, zoom out one step to reach the folder
above.
.TP
.B Esc
Close the facts, the help, the filter prompt or the mark pane; else clear
the filter; else go up the path.
.TP
.B z
Zoom the map to the folder under the cursor (a file's folder).
.TP
.B Z
Zoom out one step.
.TP
.B 0
Zoom all the way out.
.TP
.BR g ", " Home
The first sibling, or the first row.
.TP
.BR G ", " End
The last sibling, or the last row.
.TP
.BR PgUp ", " PgDn
A page of siblings or rows up, or down.
.TP
.BR 1 ", " 2 ", " 3 ", " 4
The treemap, the list, the largest files, the types.
.TP
.B s
The next sort of the siblings and the list: size, name, modified (the
newest change under an entry first), count of files.
.TP
.B t
Labels on the tiles of at least 8\(mu2 cells, on or off.
.TP
.B c
The next colour mode:
.BR type ,
.BR folder ,
.BR level ,
.BR age ,
.BR lscolors ,
as
.B \-\-color\-by
names them.
.TP
.B p
The next theme pack.
.TP
.B /
Filter by a glob, matched as the list verbs'
.B \-\-glob
matches, against the path under the scan's root
.RB ( *.zip
at any depth,
.B /Library/*
from the root): the map, the list and the largest files keep what
matches, or lies in a folder that does.
.B Enter
applies it, an empty glob clears it, and
.B Esc
leaves the prompt.
.TP
.B Tab
Focus the next pane: the map (or the table in its place), the siblings,
the legend.
The arrow keys,
.BR g ,
.BR G ,
the page keys and
.B Enter
act in the pane in focus; the other keys act on the map.
.TP
.B Shift\-Tab
Focus the previous pane.
.TP
.B i
The selection's full facts;
.B i
again or
.B Esc
closes them.
.TP
.B y
Copy the selection's path to the clipboard with OSC 52, except inside
tmux or screen, in Apple's Terminal and on the Linux console; the key line
shows the path either way.
.TP
.B o
Show the selection in the file manager, selected in its folder and never
opened: Finder on macOS; elsewhere the freedesktop file manager, else
.B xdg\-open
on its folder.
.TP
.B d
Mark the selection for the Trash, or unmark it.
.TP
.B D
Open the mark pane.
.TP
.B r
Scan the viewer's root again (not the zoomed folder alone) with the saved
scan's options, beside the viewer, and reload when the new scan is saved,
keeping the view, the filter, the marks and the path to the selection.
.TP
.B ?
List the keys;
.B ?\&
or
.B Esc
closes the list.
.TP
.BR q ", " Ctrl\-C
Quit, giving the terminal back.
.SS "Moving to the Trash"
.B d
marks the selection, and unmarks it when it is marked.
A folder's mark covers everything under it: marking a folder drops the
marks inside it, and an entry inside a marked folder is not marked again.
The map's root cannot be marked.
.B D
opens the mark pane: the plan
.B clean
makes of the marked paths, against the saved scan the viewer shows (see
CLEANING UP), in the table, the line and the question
.B clean
writes on a terminal.
The plan is made beside the viewer and takes as long as a scan of the
marked folders;
.B Esc
gives it up.
Typing
.B yes
in full, then
.BR Enter ,
moves the items to the Trash; any other answer closes the pane and moves
nothing.
A plan that refuses an item moves nothing, as
.B clean
does without
.BR \-\-skip\-refused .
The viewer never deletes: it has no
.BR \-\-permanent .
.PP
What moved stays on the map as a freed tile until the viewer closes, and
neither it nor a folder that holds it can be marked again before a
rescan.
When the viewer closes, stderr says what it moved, with the
.B index_untouched
warning: the saved scans count what moved until
.B duscope rescan
measures the tree again.
A map that no saved scan holds
.RB ( "scan \-i"
without
.BR \-\-save )
cannot be planned, and the pane says so.
